# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# NVIDIA API (build.nvidia.com / NIM) — credentials + connection manual
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SAVE THIS FILE AS:  ~/NVIDIA_API_KEY.env
# (the coding-peers and delegating-to-external-models skills look for it there)
#
# FOR AI ASSISTANTS (Claude, Codex, etc.) — INSTRUCTIONS:
#   This file is a dotenv: parse the KEY=VALUE lines (ignore # lines).
#   Connect with the OpenAI-compatible endpoint:
#     POST ${NVIDIA_BASE_URL}/chat/completions
#     Header: Authorization: Bearer ${NVIDIA_API_KEY}
#   The SAME key works for every model listed below.
#   ⚠️ The key contains '-' and '_': take it COMPLETE, to end of line.
#     If you extract it with a regex use: nvapi-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+  ON THIS ONE LINE
#     (never join lines before extracting — the ID below would get glued on).
#
# ── ✅ COPY-PASTE: the CORRECT way to load the key ───────────────────────────
#   Bash / zsh — take EVERYTHING after the first '=' on that ONE line:
#     export NVIDIA_API_KEY=$(grep -E '^NVIDIA_API_KEY='  ~/NVIDIA_API_KEY.env | head -1 | cut -d= -f2-)
#     export NVIDIA_BASE_URL=$(grep -E '^NVIDIA_BASE_URL=' ~/NVIDIA_API_KEY.env | head -1 | cut -d= -f2-)
#   Smoke test (must print exactly 'HTTP 200'):
#     curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}\n' "$NVIDIA_BASE_URL/chat/completions" \
#       -H "Authorization: Bearer $NVIDIA_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#       -d '{"model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hi"}],"max_tokens":4}'
#   Python (no dependencies):
#     import re; from pathlib import Path
#     env = dict(re.findall(r'(?m)^(\w+)=(.*)$', (Path.home()/'NVIDIA_API_KEY.env').read_text()))
#     key, base = env['NVIDIA_API_KEY'], env['NVIDIA_BASE_URL']   # also env['NVIDIA_MODEL_GLM']
#   Quick validation: the key is ONE line, starts with 'nvapi-', and the smoke
#   test returns HTTP 200. If it does NOT return 200, the extraction broke it.
#
# ── ❌ EXTRACTIONS THAT FAIL (all return HTTP 403) ───────────────────────────
#   The key contains '-', '_' and often a DOUBLE dash in the middle. So:
#     · grep -oE 'nvapi-[A-Za-z0-9]+'   → cuts at the 1st '-'   (len 8)  → 403
#     · cut -d-   /   split('-')        → splits on the dashes  (len 8)  → 403
#     · join lines then apply a regex   → glues the line below  (len 85) → 403
#   GOLDEN RULE: always extract from the single line starting with
#   'NVIDIA_API_KEY=' and take to end of line. Never use '-' as a delimiter
#   and never strip newlines before extracting.

NVIDIA_API_KEY=nvapi-PASTE_YOUR_OWN_KEY_HERE
NVIDIA_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1

# ── Models available with this key ──────────────────────────────────────────
#   The SAME key works for ALL of them.
NVIDIA_MODEL_MINIMAX=minimaxai/minimax-m3
NVIDIA_MODEL_GLM=z-ai/glm-5.2

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# HOW TO USE GLM-5.2 (z-ai/glm-5.2) — reasoning + heavy coding (Zhipu) — DEFAULT
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#   ⚠️ SAFEGUARD — WHEN to delegate (and when NOT to):
#     Delegate ONLY if the task meets BOTH:
#       (a) ≥80 estimated lines of new/changed code, OR touches 3+ files
#       (b) the spec is already clear and complete (no discovery on the way)
#     Otherwise DO IT YOURSELF: the fixed cost of delegating (building context,
#     scanning, applying, verifying) exceeds the gain on a 10-15-line fix.
#   - Tested params (HTTP 200): temperature 1, top_p 1, max_tokens up to 16384,
#     seed 42. The `seed` pins the output → useful for deterministic repeats.
#   - ✅ NOT subject to MiniMax's ~60s cap (MEASURED): streamed 104s straight
#     with no cut and closed clean (finish_reason=stop, [DONE]). The output limit
#     is max_tokens (16384), NOT 60s → no need to split the task.
#   - ✅ Huge input: accepted 180K prompt tokens (~810K chars) in 10s. Not
#     size-bound in practice (tested 22K / 90K / 180K input tokens, all 200).
#   - ⚠️ SLOW: ~20 tokens/s. A long output takes real minutes (16384 tok ≈
#     13-15 min). So use "stream":true and a WIDE timeout: curl -N --http1.1 -m 900
#     (MiniMax is fine with -m 60; GLM needs MUCH more because it's slow, not
#     because it gets cut). GLM's real cost is WALL-TIME from throughput, not a cap.
#   - ⚠️ Transient 502 "Bad Gateway / Upstream request failed" seen once:
#     retry ONCE (same rule as an empty/drop on MiniMax; don't loop).
#   - Do NOT send empty content ("content":"") — send real text.
#
#   curl -N --http1.1 -m 900 "$NVIDIA_BASE_URL/chat/completions" \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $NVIDIA_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"..."}],
#          "temperature":1,"top_p":1,"max_tokens":16384,"seed":42,"stream":true}'


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# HOW TO USE MINIMAX-M3 (minimaxai/minimax-m3) — multimodal (text/image/video)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#   - Same endpoint and SAME key as GLM (Authorization: Bearer $NVIDIA_API_KEY).
#   - Tested params: temperature 1.0, top_p 0.95, max_tokens up to 8192.
#   - The server's ~60s cap DOES apply to MiniMax (unlike GLM): split the task
#     if it won't finish in <60s (see "Notes" below). Faster than GLM on small jobs.
#   - Do NOT send empty content ("content":"") — send real text.
#   - Multimodal: for images/video, make "content" a LIST of parts
#     (public URL or base64 data URI):
#       "content": [
#         {"type":"text","text":"Describe this."},
#         {"type":"image_url","image_url":{"url":"https://example.com/img.jpg"}},
#         {"type":"video_url","video_url":{"url":"https://example.com/vid.mp4"}}
#       ]
#     base64:  {"type":"image_url","image_url":{"url":"data:image/png;base64,<B64>"}}
#
#   curl "$NVIDIA_BASE_URL/chat/completions" \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $NVIDIA_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"model":"minimaxai/minimax-m3","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"..."}],
#          "temperature":1.0,"top_p":0.95,"max_tokens":8192}'


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DATA POLICY (MANDATORY for every assistant that uses this file)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#   Every payload sent to these models leaves the user's machine → it is a
#   data boundary. BEFORE each send, scan the complete prompt for:
#     · Secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, connection strings, .env
#     · Personal data: real names, emails, phones, national IDs, birth dates,
#       candidate/client records
#     · Proprietary IP: identifiable business logic, internal prompts, legal
#       documents, DB exports
#   If any of that is present: (1) REDACT with placeholders (<API_KEY_1>,
#   <PERSON_1>) keeping the mapping LOCAL only, (2) TELL the user exactly what
#   you hid alongside the send, (3) RESTORE the real values in the response
#   before using it. If redaction would break the task (the sensitive thing IS
#   the task), STOP and let the user choose.
#   GLM is the default peer (most capable); MiniMax for multimodal tasks.
#   This file's key goes in the Authorization header, NEVER inside a prompt.
#   Canonical skill: ~/.codex/skills/delegating-to-external-models/
#   Mirror, when maintained: ~/.claude/skills/delegating-to-external-models/
#
# ── Notes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   - Model catalog (public, no key needed): GET $NVIDIA_BASE_URL/models
#   - A 403 "Authorization failed" = invalid/truncated key (check the extraction)
#     or a key generated in NGC without entitlement — generate keys from the
#     model's own page on build.nvidia.com.
#   - ⚠️ curl: (16) "Error in the HTTP2 framing layer" / hangs ~60s → HTTP 000:
#     that's HTTP/2 negotiation, NOT the key. Force HTTP/1.1 with  --http1.1
#     (measured: with HTTP/2 it hung, with --http1.1 it returned 200 in ~34s).
#   - ⚠️ Long generations on MiniMax get cut: there is a server-side ~60s cap.
#     Non-stream → "empty reply" (curl 52); stream → partial (curl 18); sometimes
#     HTTP 200 with EMPTY content under load. Mitigate: use "stream":true, validate
#     content is NOT empty, lower max_tokens / split the task into calls that
#     finish in <60s (raising -m does NOT help, the cap is server-side).
#     Use curl -N --http1.1, NOT urllib (urllib gives RemoteDisconnected on cold start).
#     GLM-5.2 is EXEMPT from this cap — see its section above.
